\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {S2}{\ignorespaces Evidence for the depth-layer boundaries at EI, KGI and CI. Left: 10-year mean profiles of potential temperature, salinity, potential density anomaly (\(\mitsigma _\mittheta \)) and dissolved oxygen, with water-mass markers (dashed): deepest winter mixed layer (90th percentile of daily mixed layer depth), Winter Water temperature minimum (December--March mean profile), main pycnocline (maximum density gradient below the winter mixed layer), and the warm-water temperature maximum. Solid lines: boundaries of the three most coherent depth groups. Right: correlation between depths of daily temperature--salinity anomalies (seasonal cycle removed) over the 10-year record, with the coherence boundaries (CONISS clustering) and species depths (ticks). The adopted layers are 0--100, 100--500 and 500--1000 m.}}{19}{figure.caption.12}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig-layers}{{S2}{19}{Evidence for the depth-layer boundaries at EI, KGI and CI. Left: 10-year mean profiles of potential temperature, salinity, potential density anomaly (\(\sigma _\theta \)) and dissolved oxygen, with water-mass markers (dashed): deepest winter mixed layer (90th percentile of daily mixed layer depth), Winter Water temperature minimum (December--March mean profile), main pycnocline (maximum density gradient below the winter mixed layer), and the warm-water temperature maximum. Solid lines: boundaries of the three most coherent depth groups. Right: correlation between depths of daily temperature--salinity anomalies (seasonal cycle removed) over the 10-year record, with the coherence boundaries (CONISS clustering) and species depths (ticks). The adopted layers are 0--100, 100--500 and 500--1000 m}{figure.caption.12}{}}
